  "slug": "enrichment-tool-gene-ontology-bbff",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and papers add computational steps that recover more marker and geometric information from existing tissue and omics measurements.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational inference that extracts more from measurements already in hand, with each item adding a model layer between raw spatial or single-cell data and the biological quantity of interest. CLEAR-AF separates tissue autofluorescence from specific signal in cyclic multiplexed immunofluorescence using a polar coordinate transformation and Gaussian mixture models. Compressed sensing (CISI) extended to imaging mass cytometry recovers 16 marker proteins from 8 composite channels, with mean Pearson r about 0.8. Generative diffeomorphic mapping aligns multimodal whole-brain datasets to an atlas via MAP estimation, quantifying cell density and geometric variation. CR-UOT addresses matching: it lets the ground cost vary within unbalanced optimal transport to align heterogeneous single-cell omics profiles. What is new in each is the specific mathematical formulation, applied to different data modalities rather than a shared benchmark.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-11-29"
}
